"JRBrown" <juliana.rb.brown@gmail.com> writes:

> There are two types of food-derived poisoning:
> a) the kind from intrinsically poisonous corpses ("That must have been
> poisonous!"), which reduces hitpoints and strength.
> b) food poisoning from spoiled corpses ("That meat was tainted! You
> feel deathly sick."), which is much more serious (a delayed
> instadeath, in fact).
> 
> Tinned food is never the former type of poisonous regardless of
> contents (which makes tins a good way to get poison resistance), 

This is correct.

> but
> *can indeed* cause food poisoning if the corpse was already old enough
> when tinned - unless the tin was blessed, in which case it won't cause
> food poisoning regardless of the age of the corpse. Note that blessed
> tinning kits will produce only blessed tins, so you can safely eat
> tinned giant mummies if you bless your tinning kit first.

This is incorrect - tinned food can never be tainted.  It can be
rotten, but this is relatively harmless.  Eating a rotten tin has the
same effect as a rotten egg or (sometimes) a tripe ration.  You will
feel "mildly nauseated", be confused and stunned for a while, and
vomit.  IIRC blessed tins can never be rotten.
